c2204737-36b2-5b9a-90d0-d28e9ef48d69.description:
This room is a typical example of *Galleria* (the second one built in Palazzo Pubblico after the Galleria Vidoniana commissioned by Cardinal Pietro Vidoni in 1655) and of Baroque *Quadratura*, a technique for which the Bolognese school was world famous during the 17th and 18th centuries. The skilled use of perspective creates the illusion of an open architectural space, framed by pillars, balconies, banisters and groups of heraldic trophies resting on fake bases. Two double rows of pillars support a fake cornice and a vault in which are depicted scenes with allegorical subjects:  
1) *Minerva* (goddess of wisdom) and Mars (god of war) lead *Fame* to announce the Virtues and the glory of Bologna to the world;  
2) The personification of Bologna, standing on a chariot pulled by two lions, which symbolize the power of the City, is guided by Cybele (goddess of nature) to the glory of Olympus, where Jupiter welcomes her;  
3) *Bacchus*, *Pomona* and *Ceres* (alluding to wine, fruit and harvest) symbolize the richness of Bolognese land.  
On each of the smaller sides are depicted the four *Civic Virtues* advising the Bolognese Senate and the heraldic symbols of the “Governo Misto” (a government composed by members of the Bolognese aristocracy and by the Legate, representative of the Papacy). The City coat of arms is supported by two female figures (*Harmony* and *Fidelity*) and by panoplies of objects alluding to papal and local government. On the opposite side sit the personifications of *Prudence* and *Vigilance*, next to the symbols of the *Arts*.

